<Base title="Relational databases: an introduction" description="How tables, queries, constraints and transactions help an application work with shared information—and which choices they leave to you.">
  <meta slot="head" name="robots" content="noindex" />
  <article class="visual-essay relational-introduction">
    <header class="essay-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage · Working draft</p>
      <h1>Relational databases</h1>
      <p class="dek">An order connects a customer, several products, and a promise to deliver them. How do we keep those facts useful as the application changes?</p>
      <div class="essay-meta"><span>Introduction and overview</span><a href="/learn/databases/choosing/#relational">Back to the field guide ↗</a></div>
    </header>

    <p>A customer opens their order history. Someone in the warehouse prints a packing list. The shop’s owner wants to know which products sold this month. Each needs a different answer from the same purchases.</p>
    <p>We could keep a separate copy of the information for each screen and report. But then every new purchase, cancellation, and correction has several places to go. A relational database lets us keep facts in tables and combine them when we ask a question. We decide how to represent those facts and which rules they must obey; the database gives us ways to query them and change them together.</p>
    <p>Let’s follow one order far enough to see what that buys us, and where we still have decisions to make.</p>

    <section id="tables">
      <h2>What belongs in an order?</h2>
      <p>Ada buys two blue mugs and a bowl. We need to record who bought them, which products they are, how many of each she ordered, and what she paid.</p>
      <p>A <strong>table</strong> holds records of the same kind. Each record is a row; its named fields are columns. Our customers table has a row for Ada. The products table has a row for the blue mug and another for the bowl. These products exist whether Ada buys them or not.</p>
      <p>Give each customer and product an identifier that distinguishes it from the others. Ada is customer <code>C4</code>; the blue mug is product <code>P7</code>. Her order, <code>O12</code>, records her customer ID. A separate <strong>order line</strong> records each product in the order: one line for two mugs, another for one bowl. Both lines carry the order ID, so we can find them together.</p>
      <p>There are four tables here: customers, products, orders, and order lines. The drawing follows just the mug line through them.</p>
      <SketchRelational />
      <p>We declare that unique identifier as the table’s <strong>primary key</strong>. An order can refer to Ada by that key instead of keeping its own copy of her customer record. Another order can refer to the same customer. We have separated the facts without losing the connection between them.</p>
      <p>This arrangement is part of the database’s <strong>schema</strong>: the tables, their columns, and the rules about their contents. Designing a schema means deciding what each record represents. That decision matters more than how neatly we draw the tables.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="queries">
      <h2>Bringing the facts back together</h2>
      <p>The packing list needs product names and quantities. Our order lines contain product IDs, so we need to match each one to its product. Matching records across tables like this is called a <strong>join</strong>.</p>
      <p>For order O12, the line containing P7 matches the blue mug’s product row. The line containing P8 matches the bowl’s. The answer is two blue mugs and one bowl. Each product ID identifies one product, so each line has one product to match.</p>
      <p>We ask for this using <strong>SQL</strong>, the query language used by PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server, and many other databases. In the example below, <code>JOIN … ON</code> describes which IDs must match, <code>WHERE</code> selects Ada’s order, and <code>SELECT</code> names the fields to return. We’ve included the price paid too, so the result can supply the item details for her receipt.</p>
      <RelationalQueryExample />
      <p>The query describes the answer we want. It doesn’t say which pieces of stored data to read first or how to find the matching records. The database engine chooses a way to execute it. That separation gives us room to ask new questions about existing records, though it doesn’t make every question cheap. A monthly report and a single packing list can require very different amounts of work.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="history">
      <h2>Today’s price is a different fact</h2>
      <p>Ada paid €18 for each mug. A week later, the shop raises the catalogue price to €20. Her receipt must still say €18.</p>
      <p>That’s why the order line stores the price paid, even though the product also has a price. One describes a past purchase; the other describes what the shop charges now. Updating the product changes the current price in one place, while the earlier purchase keeps its meaning.</p>
      <p>We made a different choice for the product name: the query reads it from the current product record. If we need a receipt to preserve the exact wording used at purchase, we must record that wording as well. Following a reference gets us the fact it points to now; preserving history requires an explicit design.</p>
      <p>Separating shared facts can save us from updating many copies. Keeping a historical snapshot can be equally important. Table design starts with what the information means and how it will change.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="constraints">
      <h2>Rules every writer has to follow</h2>
      <p>Suppose a mistake in an import creates an order line for product P99, which doesn’t exist. A well-behaved checkout form wouldn’t allow it, but the import bypassed that form.</p>
      <p>We can put rules in the schema instead. Require every order line to have a product ID, then declare a <strong>foreign key</strong> requiring that ID to match an existing product. We can also require a quantity to be present and greater than zero. These rules are called <strong>constraints</strong>. When configured, the database checks them even if the change comes from a different application or an import script.</p>
      <p>Constraints enforce the rules we actually express. A positive quantity doesn’t prove the warehouse has enough stock. A valid product ID doesn’t prove a payment succeeded. The application still has to decide what a valid purchase means, and which parts the database can enforce. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-constraints.html">PostgreSQL’s constraint guide</a> shows the rules available and their limits.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="checkout">
      <h2>Keeping related changes together</h2>
      <p>Recording Ada’s purchase changes more than one row. We create an order, add its lines, and reduce the available stock. If saving a line fails after we’ve reduced the stock, we shouldn’t leave the shop with fewer mugs and no complete order explaining where they went.</p>
      <p>A <strong>transaction</strong> groups these changes into one unit. To <strong>commit</strong> is to accept the changes together; to <strong>roll back</strong> is to discard them. For Ada’s order, a successful commit records the purchase and reserves its stock. A rollback discards both the new order records and the stock changes. This all-or-nothing property is called <strong>atomicity</strong>.</p>
      <p>There is another problem if two customers try to buy the last mug. Both might read “one available” before either changes it. Putting each checkout in a transaction doesn’t, by itself, tell the database how to resolve that conflict. The application must use the database’s concurrency controls appropriately and handle rejected attempts. That is a separate question from keeping one checkout’s changes together.</p>
      <p>The boundary also matters. Sending an email or charging a payment service isn’t automatically part of the database transaction. And if the connection disappears before the application receives the answer, it may not know whether a commit succeeded. Those cases need their own handling; blindly repeating the whole purchase can do it twice. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-transactions.html">The transaction tutorial</a> is a useful starting point for the database’s part of the job.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="choice">
      <h2>Choosing a relational database</h2>
      <p>For this shop, the appeal is the combination. We can represent customers and purchases separately, ask different questions across them, enforce shared rules, and keep related changes together. These needs also appear in booking systems, business applications, and many other services where information is shared and repeatedly updated.</p>
      <p>There is work attached to those capabilities. A schema takes thought and changes as the application evolves. Queries can do more work than their short SQL suggests. Concurrent updates can wait or conflict. An extra lookup structure, called an <strong>index</strong>, can help a query find records while giving writes more information to maintain. We need to understand the workload as well as the data.</p>
      <p>The relational model doesn’t settle how the engine stores records or where it runs. PostgreSQL and MySQL’s InnoDB make different choices about how table rows and indexes are arranged. SQLite runs inside the application process; a database server is a different deployment choice. These systems share useful ideas without having identical behaviour.</p>
      <p>Nor are the product categories sealed off. A relational engine may also support documents, text search, or vector retrieval. The useful question is whether its particular implementation fits the work. A capability in your existing database may save you another system to operate; a separate system may serve a workload better, at the cost of moving and maintaining another copy of the data.</p>
      <p>Ada’s order brings us back to the starting point. Her order history, the warehouse’s packing list, and the owner’s sales report can all draw on the same recorded purchases. The next layer is understanding how to design those records well, make their queries efficient, and keep their changes correct as more people use the application.</p>
    </section>

